column1:
27:11|When its branches dry, they are broken;
women come and light fires with them.
For this people has no understanding;
so its Maker will not show it compassion,
and its Former will not favor it.|Preserve women gathering fuel and withheld compassion.

31:3|The Egyptians are human, not God;
their horses are flesh, not spirit.
When the **LORD** stretches out his hand,
the helper will stumble and the helped will fall;
together they will all perish.|Keep flesh/spirit contrast and both parties’ failure.

31:5|Like birds in flight,
the **LORD** of hosts will shield Jerusalem;
shielding, he will deliver it;
passing over, he will rescue it.”|Keep bird metaphor and passing-over verb.

33:9|The land mourns and droops;
Lebanon is ashamed and withers.
Sharon has become like the Arabah;
Bashan and Carmel shake off their leaves.|Preserve four named regions and desert comparison.

33:23|Your ropes hang loose;
they do not hold the mast firm or spread the sail.
Then abundant plunder will be divided;
even the lame will take the spoil.|Preserve abrupt ship address and weak sharing plunder.

34:10|Night and day it will not go out;
its smoke will rise forever.
From generation to generation it will lie waste;
no one will pass through it forever and ever.|Preserve repeated unending desolation.

41:17|The poor and needy seek water, but there is none;
their tongues are parched with thirst.
I, the **LORD**, will answer them;
I, Israel’s God, will not abandon them.|Keep material need and two assurances.

43:2|When you pass through water, I am with you;
through rivers, they will not sweep you away.
When you walk through fire, you will not be scorched;
the flame will not burn you.|Keep passage through danger rather than avoidance of it.

44:26|confirming my servant’s word
and fulfilling my messengers’ counsel;
saying of Jerusalem, ‘She will be inhabited,’
and of Judah’s cities, ‘They will be rebuilt;
I will raise her ruins,’|Preserve servant singular, messengers plural, and restoration promises.

46:4|Even to old age I am he;
even when you are gray I will bear you.
I made you, and I will carry you;
I will bear you and rescue you.|Preserve repeated first-person carrying across life stages.

48:5|So I told you long ago;
before it happened I made you hear,
lest you say, ‘My idol did these things;
my carved image and cast image commanded them.’|Retain prediction’s anti-idolatry rationale.

48:8|You had not heard, you had not known;
your ear had not been opened long ago.
For I knew you would betray, betray;
from the womb you were called a rebel.|Keep three negatives and emphatic betrayal.

49:1|Listen to me, coastlands;
hear me, peoples far away.
The **LORD** called me from the womb;
from inside my mother he spoke my name.|Retains prenatal calling and distant maritime audience without naming the speaker beyond the poem.

51:5|My righteousness is near;
my salvation has gone out.
My arms will judge the peoples.
The coastlands wait for me;
they put their hope in my arm.|Keeps plural arms and singular arm, righteousness-salvation pairing.

57:8|Behind the door and doorpost
you placed your memorial.
Leaving me, you uncovered yourself and climbed up;
you widened your bed and made a pact with them.
You loved their bed;
you gazed on their nakedness.|Retains sexualized covenant infidelity; literal hand euphemism documented.

58:13|If you restrain your foot on the Sabbath
from pursuing your own interests on my holy day,
if you call the Sabbath a delight
and the **LORD**’s holy day honorable,
if you honor it by not following your own ways,
pursuing your own interests or talking business,|Retains repeated own-interests language; final speech idiom interpretation noted.

59:6|Their webs cannot become clothing;
they cannot cover themselves with what they make.
Their works are works of wrongdoing;
acts of violence are in their hands.|Retains work repetition and failed clothing image.

59:7|Their feet run to evil;
they hurry to shed innocent blood.
Their thoughts are thoughts of wrongdoing;
devastation and destruction mark their highways.|Keeps innocent victims and repeated wrongdoing from previous verse.

59:8|They do not know the way of peace;
there is no justice in their tracks.
They have made their paths crooked;
no one walking them knows peace.|Preserves peace frame and path metaphor throughout.

60:5|Then you will see and beam with joy;
your heart will tremble and open wide.
For the sea’s abundance will turn toward you;
the nations’ wealth will come to you.|Keeps physical heart reaction and maritime wealth.

60:17|Instead of bronze I will bring gold;
instead of iron, silver;
instead of wood, bronze;
instead of stone, iron.
I will appoint Peace as your overseer
and Righteousness as your taskmaster.|Preserves all four substitutions and transformed authority titles.

62:1|For Zion’s sake I will not be silent;
for Jerusalem’s sake I will not rest,
until her righteousness shines like dawn
and her salvation burns like a torch.|Leaves first-person speaker unnamed and keeps two light images.

63:3|“I have trodden the press alone;
no one from the peoples was with me.
I trod them in my anger
and trampled them in my fury.
Their lifeblood spattered my clothes;
I stained all my garments.|Preserves violent agency and alone emphasis.

66:3|One slaughters an ox—and kills a person;
one sacrifices a lamb—and breaks a dog’s neck;
one brings a grain offering—and pig’s blood;
one burns memorial incense—and blesses an idol.
They have chosen their own ways
and delight in their offensive things.|Preserves terse paired acts; equivalence versus combined practices noted.
